ps1transfer, the computer tool of PS1 DEV CART

PS1 DEV CART is an alternative firmware for the PS1 DEV CART development cartridge for the Playstation 1, which allows data
transfer between your computer and your Playstation 1 over USB (see the README of the repository).
This cartridge must be used with a USB mini-B cable and the provided "ps1transfer" tool available for Windows 32/64bits and Linux 64bits.
It is recommended to plug the USB cable to your computer whenever and before you boot your Playstation 1 with the cartridge in ON position.

The ps1transfer tool must be executed with parameters in a command line environment.
(under windows use "cmd" or "PowerShell", under Linux use a shell like "bash")
Windows users must install FTDI D2xx USB driver: https://ftdichip.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/08/CDM212364_Setup.zip
Linux users must install libftdi1 (Under Debian: sudo apt-get install libftdi1)

v1.4 update: New software reset feature and kernel_hook contributed by david4599 https://github.com/david4599/ps1usb/

Parameters:
-u Command to Upload a specified file from your computer to a specified address in the Playstation 1 memory.
-d Command to Download a file from a specified address and size in the Playstation 1 memory to your computer in the specified file.
-spu file Write the SPU RAM (512KB) then the SPU registers (512 bytes, 0x1F801C00-0x1F801DFF) of the running program
   to file (read by the debug monitor, version 10: PS1 Dev Cart Manager's SPU RAM Viewer)
-v Command to Download the Video RAM content of the Playstation 1 to a PNG image on your computer using the following parameters: x_position y_position width height
   -v auto: the 320x240 screen on the TV, where the program shows it (the TIM viewer tells it, and holds a complete
   frame while it is read); 0,0 for other programs
   While a program runs, it is a live screenshot read from the GPU. At the PS1 DEV CART menu, it is the VRAM saved at the last boot
   (e.g. the last screen of your program after a reset or a crash).
-e Command to Transfer and Execute a specified Playstation 1 executable (PS-X EXE)
-r Reset the PS1 (will be done first if combined with actions above)
-f Specify the file name on your computer
-a Specify the target address in the Playstation 1 memory in hexadecimal
-s Specify the size of the data to transfer in decimal (only for the Download "-d" command)
-c Command to Switch in TTY console mode & PC file server after the "Execute" command.
-c off (or -coff) Execute without redirecting printf/TTY to USB, the BIOS "tty" device is kept (e.g. for serial link play)
-c sio (or -csio) Execute with a serial port "tty" driver installed, like the Net Yaroze boot disc does (open("tty:"), SIO ioctls, e.g. for link play)
-c link (or -clink) The same "tty" driver on the USB link instead of the serial port: link play without a cable,
   through the cartridges and this computer (run the program on both consoles, then -relay, see "USB link" below)
-live With -v at the menu: download the screen as it is now (by default, the VRAM saved at boot, e.g. the last screen
   of a program before a reset; while a program runs, -v is always live)
-p Specify the path of the files for the file server, require "-c" command.
   The PS1 can only open files in that folder (or the current folder without -p): absolute paths, drive names and
   ".." are refused, with a "PCDRV: ... refused" message
-pcdrvany The file server opens any path of the computer (as before the folder restriction)
-pcdrvlog The file server activity as lines (for the GUI): pcdrv=init, pcdrv=open|create fd=N mode=... name="...",
   pcdrv=close fd=N name="..." read=R written=W, pcdrv=error op=... mode=... name="..." reason="..."
-z Set device id (only relevant if you have multiple PS1 DEV CART devices connected to the PC) - the id follows the USB enumeration order and may change
-n Open the PS1 DEV CART device with the specified USB serial number (stable when using multiple PS1 DEV CART devices)
-l List the connected USB devices with their device id and serial number
   (PS1 DEV CART firmware: the serial number is also shown on the cartridge menu as "Device ID", sent by ps1transfer
   when it connects to the menu and remembered in the cartridge flash)
-m Display a TMD model with the model viewer (tmdviewer.ps-exe, built from the "tmdviewer" folder, must be next to ps1transfer)
-t TIM texture for the model viewer, can be used several times (up to 8)
-tod TOD animation for the model viewer (Sony's format, e.g. from rsdlink/Animator), up to 16
-mdllive With -m: the model viewer takes commands on the input (PS1 Dev Cart Manager), acting as its controller does:
   "tod I" (TOD I from its start), "frame F" (shown, paused), "play 0/1", "loop 0/1", "speed P" (percent),
   "rot X Y Z" (view, 4096 = 360 degrees), "dist D" (camera distance), "view" (reset), "quit". It prints
   "MDLINFO TODS DIST_MIN DIST_MAX" at the start, then "MDLSTATE TOD FRAME FIRST LAST PLAYING LOOP SPEED ROT_X ROT_Y
   ROT_Z DIST OBJECTS POLYGONS SKIPPED" each time the viewer's state changes (also with its controller)
-snd Sound file for the sound tester (soundtest.ps-exe next to ps1transfer): VH, VB, VAB, SEQ or VAG, up to 8 files
-sndlive With -snd: the sound tester takes commands on the input (PS1 Dev Cart Manager), acting as its controller does:
   "program I" (index of the programs with tones), "note N" (0-127, 60 = C4), "volume V" (0-127, also for the notes
   sounding), "play [N]" / "release" (Cross held), "seq 0/1" (SEQ stop/start), "stop" (all sounds), "vabwrite OFFSET
   HEX" (VAB editor: bytes written in the VH the tester plays, applied at once to the notes sounding), "vabread" (the
   VH: "SNDVH HEX"), "spuregs" (the SPU registers: "SNDSPU HEX", debug monitor version 10), "quit". It prints
   "SNDINFO KIND PROGRAMS VAB_PS VAB_TS VAB_VS VAG_RATE", "SNDPROGS P0 P1..." at the start, then
   "SNDSTATE PROG_INDEX PROGRAM NOTE VOLUME PLAYING SEQ BAR LOOP VOICES" each time the tester's state changes (also with
   its controller; VOICES: hexadecimal, bit n = voice n sounding)
-tim TIM file for the TIM and VRAM viewer (timviewer.ps-exe next to ps1transfer), up to 32 files
-timlive With -tim -c: the viewer takes commands on the input (PS1 Dev Cart Manager): "clut I ROW V0 V1..." writes a
   CLUT row of TIM I (16-bit colours in hexadecimal) in the viewer's copy and in VRAM, "read" prints the CLUTs as the
   console has them (TIMCLUT lines), "vram FILE" (FILE after a tab) writes the whole VRAM (1024x512, 16-bit),
   "select I ROW" shows TIM I (CLUT row ROW, -1: unchanged) on the console, "state" prints the TIM and CLUT row the
   console shows and if its text is shown ("TIMSEL I ROW TEXT"), "text 0/1" hides/shows the console's info panel and
   controls, "move I IX IY CX CY" moves TIM I in VRAM (image at IX,IY, CLUT at CX,CY, -1 -1: no CLUT; the viewer
   clears the old areas, a save on the PS1 keeps the new position), "quit"
-mcbackup Save a memory card to a 128KB image file (cardtool.ps-exe next to ps1transfer)
-mcrestore Write a memory card image (128KB raw or DexDrive .gme) to a memory card, confirmation on the PS1
-slot Memory card slot 1 or 2 for -mcbackup/-mcrestore (default 1)
-padtest Control tester (padtest.ps-exe next to ps1transfer), with -pad: both controller ports as a program sees
   them through the BIOS (device, buttons, sticks, NeGcon, mouse pointer, GunCon sight), real or emulated
-link [yaroze] Link cable tester (linktest.ps-exe next to ps1transfer), run it on both consoles. The serial port is
   used like a game using the link cable library (libcomb), or with "yaroze" like a Net Yaroze game ("tty:" driver,
   as -c sio), or with "usb" no serial port: psxcomb on the USB link, the consoles are linked through their
   cartridges and this computer (see -relay)
-relay SERIAL2 USB link relay between the cartridge of -n and the cartridge SERIAL2, until stopped (Ctrl+C, or a line
   / the end of the standard input when it is a pipe). With -gdb: the relay runs in the debug session (link play
   debugged on the cartridge of -n, both programs with -c link; firmware with the debug monitor version 12)
-r With a program to run: if the PS1 doesn't take the reset (a transfer stopped half way, e.g. a program's console
   ended), its data are read until it is quiet, then the reset again
-net host:port Connects the USB link of the running program (psxcomb USB link, or -c link) to a TCP server, until
   stopped (Ctrl+C); with -e -f program.ps-exe: executes it first, printf kept on the BIOS device (unless -c).
-prof [rate[,counter]] Profiler of the running program, until stopped (Ctrl+C): every second what limits the frame
   rate (ok, cpu, gpu, vsync: the frame misses the VBlank, capped, cd), CPU load (time outside VSync/DrawSync/CdSync...)
   and the time waiting for the VBlank, the GPU, the CD-ROM, GPU busy, frames and frame time against the budget of the
   video mode (50/60 Hz, debug monitor 7), DMA, CD-ROM busy, stack (lowest SP, deepest), RAM used and free (largest
   free block), SPU voices (most at once), and the functions where the time goes (symbols: .elf/.map/.sym next to the
   program, the symbols of a Net Yaroze ECOFF executable, or -sym); at the end, averages and peaks, the verdict, hints
   (BIOS time, printf, the function to optimise first, low RAM, SPU voices...), and the functions with their share of
   the time and their caller (seen in the return address). With -e -f program.ps-exe (or -auto): executed first, its
   free RAM filled to measure what it uses (not with data files of an auto script above the program). RAM is shown
   of the 2MB: kernel + program (all of it counted) + the free RAM written, e.g. "RAM 1980/2048 KB, 68 KB free
   (largest block 52 KB)".
   rate: samples a second (default 1000, about 4% of the program's time), counter: root counter used (default 0).
   Needs the debug monitor version 3; not during a -gdb session (works with -c sio / -c link: link play).
-nofill With -prof -e: the free RAM is not filled (RAM use not measured), e.g. data uploaded there with -u
-json With -prof: "PROF {...}" lines (PS1 Dev Cart Manager) instead of the text summary
-csv file With -prof: one line per second in a CSV file (also the waits, frame time, video mode budget and verdict)
-idle f1,f2 With -prof: more functions where the program waits (not CPU load), names or addresses as shown
-gdb [port] Debug the executable given with -f with GDB or VS Code (GDB server on localhost, default port 3333)
-frame -f game.exe Frame capture (PS1 Dev Cart Manager's Frame Inspector): the program runs in a debug session;
   "capture DRAWOTAG [VSYNC]<tab>FILE" on the input stops it when it calls DrawOTag (between two VSync calls: one
   frame; "-" instead of the address: at its GPU DMA) and prints the GPU packets of its ordering tables ("FRMPKT N ADDR
   W0 W1..."), the draw area and offset (GP1 10h), and writes the frame drawn (VRAM) to FILE. Also "pad", "padport", "padstate"
-pad With -e: a controller port can be driven from the computer ("pad BITS" of -memlive or -frame: PsyQ pad bits
   in hexadecimal, "pad off"; "padport 0" port 1 (default), "padport 1" port 2; "padstate": "MEMPAD READY BUF1 BUF2
   MONVER"; "padtype ID" another device: 41 digital pad, 73 DualShock, 53 analog joystick, 23 NeGcon, 12 mouse,
   63 GunCon, then "pad BITS" its reply's buttons, "padaxes HEX" its 4 other bytes (sticks RX RY LX LY, NeGcon twist I
   II L, GunCon X Y 16-bit), "padmove DX DY" mouse motion). For programs reading their pads through the BIOS
   (InitPAD); the firmware needs the debug monitor version 8, version 9 for port 2, version 11 for the other devices
-memlive Memory of the running program read/written on request ("read ADDR SIZE", "write ADDR HEX"), the remote pad
   ("pad", "padport", "padstate", "padtype", "padaxes", "padmove"), "screen FILE" (the frame being drawn, 16-bit
   pixels: "MEMSCREEN X Y W H FILE"); its printf is shown and its files served meanwhile
-hot file[@where] Hot reload a file into the running program (VRAM, SPU RAM or RAM), can be used several times
-watch With -hot: send each file again when it changes on disk, and show the printf of the program
-flash rom.rom Flash a cartridge ROM: checked first (128KB, cartridge boot header), uploaded to RAM, flashed by the
   flasher (ar_flash.ps-exe next to ps1transfer, it shows its progress on the TV and reports here), then the PS1 is
   reset and the flash is read back and compared. Exit code 0 only if the readback is identical.
-bootcd Boot the disc in the drive, like START + SELECT on the menu: the CD drive is unlocked first (burned and
   import discs, no modchip; not on Japanese consoles), then SYSTEM.CNF and the game are loaded as the BIOS does.
   A program running is reset first. The messages are on the TV.
-info Cartridge state as key=value lines: device, state (menu, running, stopped, crashed), firmware, features, monitor,
   monitor_version, escaped_answers (1: answers after a DLE, text a program prints can't be taken for them)
-rename NAME With -n SERIAL: renames the cartridge, i.e. its USB serial number (1 to 15 letters and digits), the
   name -n, -l and the PS1 menu use. Only the cartridge's USB chip (FT245R) is written, with FTDI's own EEPROM
   programming function: its settings are saved first (%LOCALAPPDATA%\PS1-DEV-CART\eeprom-backups\SERIAL-date.txt,
   read back and compared), only the serial number is changed, and everything is read back afterwards (programmed
   back as it was if anything differs). The chip then reconnects with its new name. The first rename of a cartridge
   also sets two descriptor fields FT_Prog doesn't show to FTDI's standard values (device release, USB version).
   Windows only for now (FTDI's FT_Prog or libftdi's ftdi_eeprom elsewhere).
-renameinfo With -n SERIAL: the name and the USB chip's settings as key=value lines (read only)
-renamerestore FILE With -n SERIAL: the USB chip programmed again from a backup made by -rename (name included)
-progress Transfer progress as progress=done/total lines instead of the "bytes remaining" text
-synth SPU synth (synth.ps-exe next to ps1transfer): 16 sample slots played over MIDI, commands read on the input
   (see "SPU synth" below). Combined with -r, the synth is started again even if it already runs.
The exit code is 0 on success and 1 after an error. The gui folder has a window for ps1transfer.

Developer notes:
Transfered data are secured using a CRC8 checksum each 2kbytes packet.
The available memory to upload your data or an executable in the Playstation 1 with PS1 DEV CART is from 0x80010000 to 0x801EEFFF included.
A debugging screen with registers and stack dump will show up on your Playstation 1 whenever your program crash.
The psxpcdrv folder is a library and an example to read and write files of your computer acting as a file server
(the PCDRV functions PCinit, PCopen, PCcreat, PClseek, PCread, PCwrite, PCclose over USB).
The file server has a file "@input": the lines given to ps1transfer's standard input (e.g. by the PS1 Dev Cart
Manager), which a program opens and reads to get commands while the file server runs; a read returns at once with what
has arrived (0 bytes if nothing). The PS1 DEV CART VIDEO TESTER (ps1-tools/videotest) takes its commands this way.
You can deactivate the cartridge by toggling the top switch to off (right position),
the Playstation 1 will then boot normally but the USB hardware will remain usable,
once the Playstation 1 booted you can switch the cartridge back on to allow the flash chip to be updated by a custom program (for example if you bricked your cartridge).


Net Yaroze compatibility:
Net Yaroze games run as the Net Yaroze tools make them, no conversion needed. You need the Net Yaroze library,
libps.exe, from your Net Yaroze boot disc (MON\LIBPS.EXE).

With the "auto" script of siocons, which loads the program and its data files:
ps1transfer -auto auto -y libps.exe
It reads "local load main" (the program), "local dload file address" (a data file, uploaded at its address) and
"local auto file" (another script); "go" and the other siocons commands are skipped. File names are relative to the
folder of the script ('\' or '/'). Each data file must fit below 0x801EF000 (the cartridge firmware, which receives
the files, is above) and must not overlap the library, the program or another file.

Or the program alone, the ECOFF executable made by the Net Yaroze compiler:
ps1transfer -e -f main -y libps.exe
Its sections are uploaded at their addresses (the program clears its .bss itself, as with the boot disc).

A game converted with yarexe (psx.exe) is a PS-X EXE that already includes the library: run it like any program,
without -y:
ps1transfer -e -f psx.exe
A program converted with eco2exe (a PS-X EXE without the library) still needs -y. "-y" also accepts the library
stripped of its 2048 bytes header (libps.bin), which is uploaded at 0x80010000.
With -y, the start code of the library (libps.exe + 0x30: callbacks, pads...) runs before the program, like with the
Net Yaroze boot disc: a small stub just below the program calls it, then jumps to the program entry point. Without it
the pads read "no controller" (the program only gets the pad buffers with GetPadBuf, the start code gives them to the
BIOS).

The Net Yaroze library has to be uploaded again after each reset of the PS1 (the cartridge flash is only 128KB, so it
can not be stored in the firmware, and the PS1 DEV CART firmware uses this memory area at boot).

The Net Yaroze boot disc also installs its own "tty" driver on the serial port (used by printf and by open("tty:"),
e.g. for link play between two consoles). Use "-c sio" to get the same behaviour with the cartridge:
ps1transfer -e -f main.exe -y libps.exe -c sio
With "-c sio", printf text is sent to the serial port like on a Net Yaroze, not to your computer.


Live debugging (while your program runs, no reset needed):
Read memory, e.g. a variable at 0x800A1234:	ps1transfer -d -f var.bin -a 0x800A1234 -s 4
Write memory (poke):						ps1transfer -u -f value.bin -a 0x800A1234
Live screenshot of the displayed buffer:	ps1transfer -v 0 0 320 240 -f screen.png
The program is paused during the transfer (it runs in the kernel hook). Transfers use 2KB packets, a screenshot of
320x240 takes about 0.5s (full VRAM 2.3s). The GPU must not be receiving a command from the CPU when the program is interrupted
(programs drawing with DMA ordering tables, like libgs/libgpu DrawOTag, are fine).


Symbols:
The executable run with -e is remembered, and the symbol file with the same name next to it (.map, .sym or .elf)
is loaded automatically by -crash, -peek/-poke and @names. -sym file gives another one.
Symbol files: GNU ld map file (-Wl,-Map=game.map), nm output (nm game.elf > game.sym), or the ELF file itself.
C++ (e.g. PsyQo programs): nm -C game.elf > game.sym gives readable names ("Game::draw(int, int)").
Addresses can be given as @symbol or @symbol+offset (-a, -peek, -poke):
ps1transfer -peek @player -s 32        hexadecimal dump with symbol names (live while the program runs)
ps1transfer -memlive                   memory read/written on request (PS1 Dev Cart Manager's watch list): commands
                                       "read ADDR SIZE" ("MEMREAD ADDR HEX"), "write ADDR HEX", "quit" on the input;
                                       the program's printf is shown and its files served meanwhile
ps1transfer -poke @lives 3 -s 1        write a 1, 2 or 4 bytes value
ps1transfer -d -f table.bin -a @table -s 1024


Crash reports:
When your program crashes, the PS1 DEV CART crash screen is displayed and still answers USB commands. Then:
ps1transfer -crash [game.map]
shows the crash cause, PC and RA with function names, the registers, and the call stack:
  Crash: Address error on load (CAUSE 30000010)
  PC  80010094  UpdatePlayer + 0xC
  RA  800100B4  UpdateGame + 0x14
  ...
  Call stack (from the function prologues, most recent first, callers shown at their call):
    #0  80010094  UpdatePlayer + 0xC
    #1  800100AC  UpdateGame + 0xC
    #2  80010100  GameLoop + 0x3C
    #3  80010080  main + 0x1C
The call stack is unwound with the function prologues read from the PS1: frame size ("addiu sp,sp,-N") and the
place where ra is saved ("sw ra,off(sp)"). If it stops early (unusual prologue), the stack is scanned for return
addresses instead: with symbols each one must follow a "jal" to the function found before it (calls through
function pointers, jalr, are marked "?").
You can also read the memory of the crashed program with -d/-peek, take a screenshot with -v, and reset with -r.
Last frame: the crash screen is drawn in the program's draw area (the buffer it was drawing), so the frame it
displayed last stays in VRAM. The report shows where (the other buffer, above or below), and saves it with -f:
ps1transfer -crash game.map -f frame.png
  Last frame displayed: VRAM 0,0 320x240
    Saved to frame.png
A program with one display buffer draws where it displays: its frame is not kept (640x480 interlaced: the report
says so; otherwise the area shown holds older VRAM contents, as the firmware can't see where a program displays).


Debugging with GDB (PS1 DEV CART firmware with the debug monitor):
ps1transfer -gdb -f game.exe          (Net Yaroze: the psx.exe made by yarexe, which includes libps)
loads the program stopped at its entry point (the PS1 is reset first if a program runs), then serves the GDB remote
protocol on localhost:3333 (-gdb 1234 for another port), with the same registers description as PCSX-Redux's GDB server:
gdb-multiarch game.elf
(gdb) target remote localhost:3333
(gdb) break main
(gdb) continue
- Breakpoints (any number), step/next/finish, backtrace, registers, memory, variables.
- Breakpoints also work in code run by interrupts (e.g. VSync callbacks): the monitor handles break instructions
  before the BIOS exception handler, so the registers of the interrupted program are kept.
- One hardware breakpoint (hbreak, also on code that can't be written) and one watchpoint (watch, rwatch, awatch,
  up to 4KB) with the COP0 debug registers. GDB checks that a watched value has really changed.
- Pause (Ctrl+C in GDB, pause in VS Code): the program stops at its next interrupt (not while interrupts are disabled).
- printf of the program is shown by ps1transfer and sent to GDB (VS Code Debug Console).
- Detach (or GDB closed): breakpoints removed, the program runs on. Kill: the PS1 is reset.
Works with -c sio / -c off (printf then goes to the serial port / BIOS device) and -n serial.
The Net Yaroze VS Code extension (https://github.com/logi-26/Net-Yaroze-WSL-Build-Environment) starts it with its
"Debug on PS1 (PS1 DEV CART)" configuration.
How it works: ps1transfer writes a break instruction at the entry point and executes the program with a debug flag.
The kernel hook then patches the exception vectors 0x80000080 (break instructions) and 0x80000040 (COP0 hardware
breakpoints). When the program stops, the debug monitor (monitor folder, in the cartridge flash) sends a request with
the address of the saved registers, and answers memory reads/writes until ps1transfer tells it to continue (the
instruction cache is flushed then). Software breakpoints are written in the program when it resumes, only what changed.
Note: the hardware breakpoints need DCIC bit 29 as well as bit 30 to trigger (tested on a console).


Hot reload (PS1 DEV CART firmware with the debug monitor, version 1):
Send textures, sounds or data to the running program, without restarting it:
ps1transfer -hot car.tim                      VRAM, at the position stored in the TIM (image and CLUT)
ps1transfer -hot car.tim@512,0                image at another VRAM position (the CLUT stays at its position)
ps1transfer -hot engine.vag@0x1010            SPU RAM address (the 48 bytes VAG header is skipped)
ps1transfer -hot bank.vb@0x1010               VAB body, or any data, in SPU RAM (address < 0x80000)
ps1transfer -hot level.bin@0x80100000         RAM (address 0x80000000...)
Several -hot files can be given. With -watch, ps1transfer keeps running: it sends a file again each time it changes
(e.g. saved by your paint program or sound editor) and shows the printf of the program, Ctrl+C to stop:
ps1transfer -hot car.tim -hot engine.vag@0x1010 -watch
The SPU address is where your program loaded the sound (e.g. the address returned by SsVabTransBody/SpuMalloc,
printed once by your program). The new data must not be bigger than the old one.
The program is paused during each transfer (in the kernel hook, at its next interrupt), the debug monitor writes the
data straight from USB to the GPU or the SPU (waiting for a running GPU/SPU DMA transfer to end first).


Link cable tester:
ps1transfer -n SERIAL1 -link
ps1transfer -n SERIAL2 -link
runs the tester on both consoles, using the serial port like a game using the link cable library libcomb (AddCOMB,
open("sio:") with O_NOWAIT, asynchronous reads and writes ended by the SIO events). The tester uses psxcomb
(psxcomb folder), our library compatible with libcomb, which gives the same signals on the cable as the PlayStation library: one byte per handshake
(the receiver toggles DTR for each byte, the sender waits for DSR to change), so it also links with a game or
program built with the original library. The reads are of the size sent each frame, so set the same packets and size
on both consoles. "BLOCKED" counts the frames whose packets were not sent because the previous write was still
waiting for the other console (flow control). A write without progress for 15 frames restarts the read and the write
("RESYNC"; the original library could stop for ever when a byte was lost, ours checks the acknowledge on a timer).
Measured at 115200 bps: about 4 KB/s each way with our library on both consoles (the original library: about
1.6 KB/s).
ps1transfer -n SERIAL1 -link yaroze
ps1transfer -n SERIAL2 -link yaroze
uses it like a Net Yaroze game instead: the serial port "tty" driver is installed ("-c sio"), then open("tty:") and
the same ioctls as YarIO. The TV shows which one runs. Each console sends numbered packets every frame (sequence, echo of the last
sequence received, test pattern, checksum) and shows: link state, CTS/DSR lines, packets sent/received/lost,
corrupted packets, throughput, round trip time (frames and ms).
Controls: Up/Down packets per frame (1-8), Left/Right packet size (8-64 bytes), L1/R1 baud rate 9600-230400
(set the same on both consoles), Cross pause sending, Start reset statistics.
Note: CTS/DSR read ON when nothing is connected (the inputs are not pulled down), only received packets prove the link.

USB link (no serial cable): both consoles linked through their cartridges and this computer:
ps1transfer -n SERIAL1 -link usb
ps1transfer -n SERIAL2 -link usb
ps1transfer -n SERIAL1 -relay SERIAL2          (runs until stopped, prints the throughput every second)
The tester uses psxcomb with CombSetLink(COMB_LINK_USB): each console has a send ring and a receive ring in RAM
(ps1-tools/linktest/usblink.h), the relay copies one console's sent bytes into the other's receive ring with the live memory
access of the kernel hook. The library publishes the address of its rings at 0x8000DF38 for the relay, and keeps the
kernel hook answering with a 1kHz timer interrupt. Measured with two consoles: 400 B/s at 8 bytes per frame, about
23 KB/s each way with the tester (4-8 x 255 bytes per frame), about 46 KB/s each way with bigger reads; round trip
about 3 frames when the relay keeps up. Bytes sent before the relay starts are dropped.
The relay and -net read and write the rings with the kernel hook's exchange (command 'X', a read or a write alone,
see below), not with D/U: the PS1 never waits for the computer's acknowledgements, so each turn takes the program a
fraction of a millisecond. With D/U (before) each turn stopped it a few milliseconds: a demo drawing most of its
frame (PS1 SLI 3 SCREENS) went from 50 to about 30 fps through -net.
Your own programs can do the same: build them with psxcomb (PSn00bSDK), call CombSetLink(COMB_LINK_USB) before
AddCOMB() (or at any time), and use open("sio:")/read/write as with the cable.

Net Yaroze programs (e.g. games using YarIO) can use the USB link unmodified: run them with "-c link" instead of
"-c sio", on both consoles, then start the relay:
ps1transfer -n SERIAL1 -y libps.exe -e -f game.exe -c link
ps1transfer -n SERIAL2 -y libps.exe -e -f game.exe -c link
ps1transfer -n SERIAL1 -relay SERIAL2
open("tty:"), read, write and the ioctls work like with the serial port driver. The relay drives each console from its
own thread, with one "exchange" transfer (kernel hook command 'X': the computer's half of the driver buffers written,
the PS1's half read) each time the kernel hook answers; the driver lets the hook answer before each read and after each
write of the program, so bytes written leave at once and bytes arriving are read in the same frame.
The PS1 answers an exchange with the program stopped (interrupts off), so the computer sends it in one USB write: the
command, its half of the buffers and both acknowledgements, the CRCs checked afterwards (a read half received wrong
isn't used, a written one is written again by the next exchange); the PS1 never waits there for the computer. Each
exchange takes about 0.75 ms of the program's time (a busy loop on both consoles: 4% of the CPU at one exchange per
frame; with an answer after each USB round trip, as before: 16%, which disturbed games' frames, e.g. flickering).
In a debug session (-gdb -relay) the debugged program can stop before its exchange is answered (its stop notification
would take the command for the computer's answer): there the command goes alone, the rest once it is answered, and an
exchange the stop got in the way of is skipped (the next one writes everything again). Escaped answers ("DLE g", after
a 'Q') are accepted too.
Frame pacing, the timing of a serial cable: each write of the program is a unit, given to the other program one unit
per frame (counted at the vertical blanks). Programs like YarIO keep only the last packet received in a frame, so two
packets arriving in the same frame would lose one and desynchronise the consoles (USB delivery isn't as regular as a
cable). Tested with a YarIO game on two consoles: stays in sync like with the cable, round trip about 3 frames
(link tester: 3.2 frames, the cable 2). The Net Yaroze pad driver's start message goes to the tty, so to the other
console (like on a real Net Yaroze, where the tty is the serial port): YarIO skips it.
The relay stops at the end of a turn on Ctrl+C or when a line is written to its input (the GUI's Stop task), never in
the middle of a transfer.

Live screenshots ("-v" while a program runs) go through the debug monitor with this firmware (monitor version 2,
'M' service), to leave kernel space for the USB link; ps1transfer chooses automatically.


Memory card backup/restore:
ps1transfer -mcbackup save.mcd [-slot 2]
reads the 1024 sectors of the card (about 12s) and writes a raw 128KB image (as used by emulators), then lists the saves.
ps1transfer -mcrestore save.mcd [-slot 2]
lists the saves of the image and writes it to the card after a confirmation on the PS1 (Cross: write, Circle: cancel).
The backup file is written by the PS1 program with the PCDRV file server, ps1transfer stops when the program has finished.

Memory card image tools (on the computer, no PS1 needed, a .bak copy is kept when an image is modified):
ps1transfer -mclist card.mcd [-icons dir]      saves with blocks, file name, title (Shift-JIS converted), icons as PNG
ps1transfer -mcexport card.mcd SAVE out.mcs    one save to a .mcs file (directory frame + blocks, PSXGameEdit format)
ps1transfer -mcimport card.mcd in.mcs          inject a save in the free blocks (directory links and checksums written)
ps1transfer -mcdelete card.mcd SAVE            delete a save (marked deleted like the PS1 does)
ps1transfer -mccopy src.mcd SAVE dst.mcd       copy a save from an image to another
ps1transfer -mcformat new.mcd                  blank formatted image
SAVE is the save file name (e.g. BESLES-12345GAME) or its first block number.
Example, inject a save in the card of slot 1 for a regression test:
ps1transfer -mcbackup slot1.mcd
ps1transfer -mcimport slot1.mcd before_bug.mcs
ps1transfer -mcrestore slot1.mcd

Saves on the card itself (PS1 DEV CART firmware): "slot1" or "slot2" in place of the image file of the commands above:
ps1transfer -mclist slot1 [-icons dir]             saves of the card in slot 1 (directory and title/icon sectors only)
ps1transfer -mcexport slot1 SAVE out.mcs           reads only the blocks of the save
ps1transfer -mcimport slot2 in.mcs                 writes only the save's blocks and the directory
ps1transfer -mcdelete slot1 SAVE                   writes only the directory frames
ps1transfer -mccopy slot1 SAVE slot2               (source and destination: a slot or an image file)
The memory card tool runs in server mode and stays running, so the next commands start at once. Writes are
confirmed on the PS1 (Cross: write, Circle: cancel). A save is imported into never used blocks first, then into the
blocks of deleted saves (they can't be undeleted any more once reused). Give -n before the command.


TIM and VRAM viewer:
ps1transfer -tim car.tim -tim font.tim -tim sky.tim ...
The TIM files are loaded in VRAM at their own position. Select changes the view:
- VRAM map: the whole VRAM, image and CLUT of each TIM outlined (selected in yellow), overlaps in red,
  viewer screen/font in grey, details and warnings of the selected TIM below
- TIM view: the selected TIM with its color depth and CLUT, palette swatches (Triangle/Cross zoom,
  L2/R2 CLUT row, D-pad scroll). Square draws it with the CLUT of another loaded TIM of the same CLUT width
  (press again for the next one, back to its own CLUT at the end). Circle opens the palette editor.
- VRAM 1:1: scroll in the raw VRAM (D-pad, R2 faster)
L1/R1 select the TIM, Start hides/shows the info panel and the controls (a clear view of the image).
Palette editor (TIM view, Circle): the image is redrawn live while the colors change.
- D-pad: entry (up/down: 16 entries), L2/R2: red/green/blue, Triangle/Cross: +/- (held: repeats)
- Square: restore the original colors of the row, Circle: exit
- Start: save the TIM with its edited palette as NAME_edit.tim, Select: reload NAME.tim (e.g. after
  editing it in a paint program; it must not be bigger than the space it had in memory)
Save and reload need the file server: add -c, the files are in the directory of the first TIM (or -p path):
ps1transfer -tim c:\tims\car.tim -tim c:\tims\sky.tim -c
Warnings: overlaps between images/CLUTs, CLUT x not multiple of 16, CLUT width not matching 4-bit (16) / 8-bit (256),
4/8-bit image crossing a texture page, outside VRAM. The viewer places its screen and font in free VRAM areas
(single buffer if only one is free), a message is shown if it has to overwrite TIM data.


Sound tester:
Play a VAB sound bank (VH + VB files, or one VAB file) and optionally SEQ music:
ps1transfer -snd bank.vh -snd bank.vb -snd music.seq
Play a VAG sample at different notes (C4 = original sample rate):
ps1transfer -snd sample.vag
Controls: Left/Right program, Up/Down note, L1/R1 octave, Cross play (while held), Circle SEQ start/stop,
Triangle/Square volume, Start stop all sounds. The file types are detected from their header (VB files: ".vb" extension).
With -sndlive (the GUI's Sound page) the same controls are on the computer, linked live with the controller's.
The files are uploaded to 0x80100000 (max 896KB, the VB must also fit in the 512KB SPU RAM), "-snd" resets the PS1 first.


SPU synth:
The PS1 as a sample synth with 16 slots: slot n plays on MIDI channel n (24 voices, pitch bend +/-2 semitones,
volume CC7, pan CC10, sustain pedal CC64, all notes off CC123). Samples are loaded while it plays.
ps1transfer -synth
starts the synth (or connects to it if it already runs, the slots are kept), then reads commands, one per line:
  midi 90 3c 64          MIDI messages in hexadecimal, several per line (here: note on, channel 1, C4, velocity 100)
  load 1 sample.vag      VAG sample to slot 1 (an optional name can follow after a tab)
  clear 1                frees slot 1
  set 1 release 40       slot setting: root (MIDI note played at the sample rate, default 60), volume, pan (0-127),
                         attack, decay, sustain, release (0-99), reverb (0/1), tune (0-100, 50 = none, in cents)
  reset                  all notes off, all slots cleared
  state                  the slots and the free SPU RAM ("slot ..." lines, "spu ...", then "end")
  quit                   (the synth keeps running on the PS1)
Each command except midi answers "ok" or "error <reason>". The GUI (Synth page) uses it for its keyboard, MIDI input
from a MIDI interface, SysEx sample upload and kits; gui/vag.py converts WAV files to VAG.
The PS1 shows the slots, the settings of the selected one and a keyboard with the notes playing. Controls: Up/Down
slot, L1/R1 setting, Left/Right change it, Cross/Triangle/Square play the slot (root, +12, -12).
How it works: the synth keeps a mailbox at 0x80100000 (MIDI ring, command, state, see ps1-tools/synth/synth.h) that
ps1transfer reads and writes with the live memory access of the kernel hook. A 1kHz timer interrupt keeps the hook
answering. A sample goes to 0x80110000 first, then the synth sends it to SPU RAM by DMA.


TMD model viewer:
Display a TMD model with its TIM textures (loaded in VRAM at the position stored in each TIM, as the TMD expects):
ps1transfer -m model.tmd -t texture1.tim -t texture2.tim
Controls: D-pad rotate X/Y, L1/R1 rotate Z, Triangle/Cross zoom in/out, Start reset view, Select hide/show the text.
The model and textures are uploaded to 0x80100000 (max 896KB). "-m" resets the PS1 first, so models can be displayed
one after the other, "ps1transfer -r" goes back to the PS1 DEV CART menu.
With TOD animations (as PsyQ's TOD sample JANKEN: te.tmd with appear.tod, jaanken.tod, n_c.tod...):
ps1transfer -m te.tmd -tod appear.tod -tod jaanken.tod -tod n_c.tod
The packets create the objects, link them to the TMD objects (rsdlink's IDs: TMD object n = ID n + 1), set their
parents (a hierarchy of coordinates) and attributes, and move them (rotation, scale, translation, absolute or from the
previous frame); light, camera and matrix packets are not used. A TOD that doesn't create its objects continues from
the end of the first TOD that does (appear.tod for the JANKEN files). The camera frames each TOD's whole motion.
Controls with TODs: Circle play/pause, L2/R2 previous/next frame (held: repeat), Square next TOD.

Any "local dload" commands from your batch transfer file can be adapted like this:
local dload bullet2.tim 800e4000 => ps1transfer -u -f bullet2.tim -a 0x800e4000


Usage examples:

Upload and execute the Playstation 1 executable "program.ps-exe":
ps1transfer -e -f program.ps-exe

Reset the Playstation 1 then Upload and execute "program2.ps-exe":
ps1transfer -r -e -f program2.ps-exe

Upload and execute a Playstation 1 executable and redirect the "printf" text of your Playstation program to your computer:
ps1transfer -c -e -f program.ps-exe

Upload and execute a a program, redirect the "printf" text and specify the path "gamedata" for the program data files for the file server:
ps1transfer -c -e -f program.ps-exe -p ./gamedata/

Upload the file "data.bin" to the address 0x80100000 in the Playstation 1 ram:
ps1transfer -u -f data.bin -a 0x80100000

Download the bios memory from the Playstation 1 and save it to the file "bios.bin":
ps1transfer -d -f bios.bin -a 0x1FC00000 -s 524288

Download the entire Video RAM from the Playstation 1 and export it as a PNG image named "vram.png":
ps1transfer -v 0 0 1024 512 -f vram.png

